docs/contributing.md	**MCP in tests and agent sessions: `photocraft-cli mcp --headless`.** Plain `photocraft-cli mcp` works in the open PhotoCraft window while the ArtCraft Launcher's agents switch is on, and then may start PhotoCraft on the developer's machine when it is closed. Scripts, tests and agents that want a session without a window pass `--headless` (or use `photocraft-cli serve`); a test that runs the CLI also gives it its own settings, launcher and flags folders (`PHOTOCRAFT_CONFIG_DIR`, `CRAFT_MCP_DIR`, `CRAFT_FLAGS_FILE`, as `crate::command()` in `apps/photocraft-cli/tests/it/main.rs` does). To drive the running app on purpose, use `--control` (`docs/development.md`).
docs/control-protocol.md	A folder left empty (and named by no flag) is the default agent folder, `Documents/ArtCraft/Agents/PhotoCraft` in the system's Documents folder (Windows, macOS, and Linux's XDG Documents folder, else `~/Documents`), created when first used; the same folder for both directions shares one capability. It comes from the suite's shared agent settings (craft-libs `craft-control`, `AgentSettings::folders`), so every Craft app has its own beside it. A default folder that can't be created is left out, so that direction fails closed. The MCP tool `agent_folders` tells an agent which folders it may use.
docs/control-protocol.md	An app started from the Dock, the Start menu or a file manager has no command line. It starts and stops the control server while it runs. With the suite's `AI-MCP` flag, ArtCraft Launcher › Settings › AI agents › *Let AI agents use Craft apps* is the one switch (for every Craft app; PhotoCraft looks at it every 2 s); without the flag, as before, a saved Control Port. Turning it on or off, or changing the port or folders in Edit › Preferences › AI Integrations, applies at once, with no restart.
docs/control-protocol.md	- **The open window** (plain `photocraft-cli mcp`): the bridge, with the address and token read before every tool call from `agent-connection.json` in the app's settings folder, with the suite's `AI-MCP` flag and while the person lets agents use the app. With agents allowed and the app closed, `integrations.whenAppClosed` decides (`launch`, the default: start it and wait up to 45 s; `headless`: headless, with `--automation-read-root` / `--automation-write-root` where given and the app's agent folder for each direction without one; `error`: fail with "PhotoCraft isn't running"), and `--when-closed` overrides it. Otherwise headless, as above (`--headless` forces that). The app publishes that file while agents may connect (the ArtCraft Launcher's switch; without the `AI-MCP` flag a saved Control Port), not with `--control`, and removes it when the connection stops; a file left by a crash fails the connection probe and counts as no app. See [MCP conventions](mcp.md#connecting-the-desktop-app-no-terminal).
docs/control-protocol.md	`ui.inspect` reports the connection as `agentLink`: `port` while agents can connect, `launchFlags` when `--control` manages it, `error`, `mcpFlag` and `launcher` (the ArtCraft Launcher's switch is on).
docs/development.md	- `agent_folders` (where the app opens and saves files for agents: `read_folder`, `write_folder`; a headless session with several read roots adds `read_folders`. With the preferences' folders empty this is the default agent folder, `Documents/ArtCraft/Agents/PhotoCraft`. In `--bridge` mode both are `null`, with a note: the app's own launch decides)
docs/development.md	- **Headless:** `photocraft-cli mcp --headless --automation-read-root <project> --automation-read-root <library> --automation-write-root <project>`. Use `--headless` in scripts, tests and agent sessions: without it `mcp` works in the open window while the ArtCraft Launcher's agents switch is on, and may start PhotoCraft (When PhotoCraft Is Closed defaults to starting it). It drives an in-process engine session with ordered read directories and one write directory.
docs/development.md	- **Live app, no flags:** with the `AI-MCP` flag, turn on ArtCraft Launcher › Settings › AI agents and add the MCP client there; plain `photocraft-cli mcp` then works in the open window. The app publishes its port and token file in `agent-connection.json` in the settings folder; `mcp` reads it on every call. Without a launcher, a scratch `CRAFT_MCP_DIR` with an `apps.json` naming `photocraft` (the format in storytold/craft-ai-integrations, crate `craft-ai-agents`) turns the switch on for a test. See [MCP conventions](mcp.md#connecting-the-desktop-app-no-terminal). Test it against a scratch settings folder with `PHOTOCRAFT_CONFIG_DIR`, which both the app and the CLI honour.
